    narr:narration """The narrative holds that [the vendor stays the system of record for reference data until the last site is live](narr:Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_36), opening from [the recurring question of when ownership actually transfers](narr:Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_34) and grounded in [the drift risk of split ownership — divergence surfacing via a mis-priced consignment weeks later](narr:Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_38). [An accountability corollary](narr:Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_39) keeps blame with the vendor through the programme, and [site-team assent](narr:Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_41) folds it into [a steering-group summary of the rollout cadence](narr:Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_42). A second session adds [the hidden dependency: both rules assume one cutover window open at a time, and would need rewriting under a parallel rollout](narr:Expr_2HalyardOpsReadiness20260915_25), drawing [a decision to stay sequential](narr:Expr_2HalyardOpsReadiness20260915_26). A third session carries [an advisor's endorsement of the reference-data call, hedged on the mirror's refresh interval](narr:Expr_6HalyardAdvisorTonyMaley20260929_25), and [a migration-plan outline restating the cadence and the vendor-holds rule](narr:Expr_6HalyardAdvisorTonyMaley20260929_3), now gathered under [the position on when reference-data authority transfers](narr:Position_ReferenceDataSourceOfRecordTransition). Nothing here records the vendor's own agreement, and the mirror's refresh interval remains unverified.""" ;
